\subsubsection{L1 Attention Pattern Analysis}
\label{sec:attention_pattern}

See Figure~\ref{fig:attention} and Table~\ref{tab:routing_gradient} (main text) for the per-head attention distribution at L1 across three conditions.
The three-level gradient (untrained 0/32, Llama-2 base 9/32, VLA-trained 25/32 instruction-dominant heads) shows that language pretraining seeds partial instruction routing and VLA fine-tuning reorganizes its temporal distribution.

\subsubsection{Multi-Layer Attention Temporal Patterns}
\label{sec:multilayer_attention}

Table~\ref{tab:multilayer_attention} extends the L1 analysis to layers L8, L16, and L31, revealing three qualitatively distinct temporal patterns.
The untrained model maintains 0/32 instruction-dominant heads at all layers.
Llama-2 base exhibits a characteristic inverted-U: heads rise from 9/32 (L1) to a peak of 25/32 at L16 ($60.2\%$ text attention), then fall to 8/32 at L31, consistent with middle-layer language processing typical of autoregressive LMs.
VLA fine-tuning produces a bookend pattern: 25/32 at L1 ($57.7\%$), dropping to 4/32 at L8 (BOS-dominant attention sink), recovering to 10/32 at L16, and re-emerging at 19/32 at L31 ($56.3\%$).
This reorganization front-loads instruction access for early visual-linguistic binding and restores it at the action-output layer.

